**Idea.** Both threads share one lock object and a counter. The odd thread prints only when the number is odd, otherwise it waits; after printing it increments the counter and calls `notifyAll()`. The even thread is symmetric. Always use `while`, not `if`, to guard `wait()`, because of spurious wakeups.

```java
class OddEven {
  private final Object lock = new Object();
  private int n = 1;
  private final int max;
  OddEven(int max) { this.max = max; }

  void print(boolean odd) {
    synchronized (lock) {
      while (n <= max) {
        while (n <= max && (n % 2 == 1) != odd) {
          try { lock.wait(); } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); return; }
        }
        if (n > max) break;
        System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " " + n++);
        lock.notifyAll();
      }
      lock.notifyAll();
    }
  }
}
// new Thread(() -> oe.print(true), "odd").start();
// new Thread(() -> oe.print(false), "even").start();
```

The `notifyAll()` at the end makes sure that the other thread wakes up and exits when the limit is reached. `wait()` releases the monitor, which is why the other thread can enter. Alternatives: two `Semaphore`s passing a permit back and forth, or a `ReentrantLock` with `Condition`s; `volatile` plus busy-waiting works but wastes CPU.

**Min swaps to group all 1s.** Let `k` = total number of 1s. The best result is the window of size `k` containing the most 1s: swaps needed = `k - maxOnesInWindow`. Slide a window of size `k`, keep a running count of 1s, update the maximum. Time O(n), space O(1). If the array is circular, double the array or use modulo indexing. Example: `[1,0,1,0,1]`, k=3, best window has 2 ones, so 1 swap.
